WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.600
Today is without question THE single strangest theory I've done in my six years of doing this crazy YouTube thing,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.780
which feels appropriate considering it's about Deadpool.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.140
So embrace the weird, and keep an open mind because if you do,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.137
you're in for one wild ride.

00:00:15.137 --> 00:00:16.420
Stick with me now.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:23.140
Is it possible that Deadpool is actually classical American author Ernest Hemingway?

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:23.960
*whoosh, pop!*

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.100
Anyone still here?

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.600
(Deadpool: *crunching* Now that's impressive!)

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:28.840
(Film Theory theme, look out for the many cameos by Deadpool)

00:00:45.140 --> 00:00:48.200
Hello, Internet! Welcome to Film Theory,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.080
where it's time for an Easter egg hunt (crack) 365 days a year! (366 on leap years)

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.120
So, yeah, I know that we just did an episode on Deadpool,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.960
which, if you haven't watched yet, you should totally watch,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:02.220
and I know that "Deadpool 2" doesn't come out until next year,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.880
but while I was researching that last episode,

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.740
I was reminded of something that initially triggered my theorist sense back when I was first watching "Logan."

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.020
I forgot about it at the time because, you know, I got kinda distracted by

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:18.360
Wolverine taking a shotgun blast to the chest within, like, literally a minute of the movie starting.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.460
But re-watching the teaser for the next Deadpool movie while researching whether or not

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:26.280
Deadpool had written his own movie reminded me of it, and thus, here we are today.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:30.400
So the short itself titled "No Good Deed" is pretty self-explanatory--

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:37.180
lots of references to other superhero tropes, obligatory "Firefly" references, a prolonged shot of a man's naked butt,(WAIT WHAT!?)

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.580
but then you get to the end and a flurry of small print runs up the screen and out of sight faster than you could say,

00:01:42.580 --> 00:01:49.220
(slow motion) "chimichanga."

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.120
Most people watching the trailer didn't bat an eye.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.520
Huh, random text at the end of a movie trailer; that's weird.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:57.380
Oh well, back to my normal life and my jumbo popcorn.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:01:58.200
Well, you know what?!

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.020
Some of us don't have normal lives!

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.060
The only jumbo popcorn bringing some of us joy

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:08.100
is trying to figure out patterns in the pop culture randomness we're bombarded with every day,

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:12.520
and then screaming about it into a microphone that we keep in our bedroom closet for fifteen minutes every week!

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.520
So, in an attempt to fill that jumbo sized emptiness in my life,

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:19.680
I went back and read the text online, only to find it was

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.080
a loose plot summary of Ernest Hemmingway's Pulitzer prize-winning novella "The Old Man and the Sea,"

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:26.760
written in the voice of what seems to be Deadpool.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.840
It's like he did a book report on it.

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:32.120
But why?  Why is it there, for the lols?

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.680
For it to just be so craaaaaaazy and random?

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.120
I refuse to believe it.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.660
These trailers take millions of dollars to make.

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:43.600
Marvel and Fox know that these movie promos get analyzed frame by frame,

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.940
which is why you have Easter eggs hidden in literally ever corner of the screen!

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.220
So something like this, which might at first glance seem like a throwaway joke

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.920
has got to have some sort of deeper meaning, but what?

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:57.980
Well, that's the problem I set out to answer today.

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:00.160
I searched around for a bit to see what people thought about this thing,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.920
and honestly, there weren't that many online analyses talking about this moment.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.680
Those few who did wrote it off as "The Old Man and the Sea" summary somehow being connected to the plot of "Logan,"

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:11.780
the movie that everybody was sitting in the theater about to see.

00:03:11.780 --> 00:03:18.200
Now, if you haven't read the book, or you read it back in high school, or didn't read the book but pretended to have read it in high school,

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.340
which is, I'm guessing, the biggest group here (you missed out),

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.500
this seems like an appropriate time to fill you in on the plot.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.400
The story is about an old fisherman named Santiago who hasn't caught a fish for months,

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:31.760
so long, in fact, that his young protégé Manolin is told by his parents to stop fishing with him.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.120
Then one day, the fisherman goes out, hooks a marlin so huge he has to tie it to the outside of his boat,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:39.900
but as he sails toward he shore, he starts to attract sharks.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.860
The sharks start to eat the marlin, and despite Santiago fighting back,

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:49.140
the sharks eat the marlin to the point that all that remains is a giant skeleton.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.840
This, understandably, makes Santiago sad, but when the other fishermen see the giant skeleton of this enormous fish,

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:58.860
they realize that Santiago got his fishing groove back and their faith in the old man is restored.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.080
This makes Santiago happy.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.280
*sarcastic clap*

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.460
Good work, Ernest Hemingway!

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.920
One of the best works of American literature right there, ladies and gentlemen

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.960
Man sucks at fishing, goes out, catches fish, gets eaten, comes back, done!

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:16.860
And THIS is why I hate American literature.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:22.300
So anyway, the online blogs' interpretation was that Santiago the fisherman, is an old, weary hero past his prime,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:27.400
just like Logan who has one final, moral victory and wins the affection of a young student,

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.300
X23, or in Logan's case, Mandolin.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.020
*laughs* Sorry, I mean, scratch that, reverse it.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:36.980
Although, I gotta say X23 hanging out with Santiago would make the book so much more interesting.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.560
But, *sighs* I don't know, it just feels off to me.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.420
Key moments and themes just don't line up.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:49.500
To avoid spoilers, let's just say that the fisherman doesn't end up in the same place Logan does at the end of "The Old Man and the Sea."

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:53.140
So, that's a pretty important detail, and there's no fish parallel in "Logan."

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.580
I mean, the most important part of the book is Santiago battling with the marlin for days,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:05:02.380
catching him, engaging in this epic struggle, and then watching it get slowly eaten away over time by the sharks.

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:06.260
To me, this doesn't match up to anything in "Logan."

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.320
"Logan" is about the relationship between Logan and X23.

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:13.260
"The Old Man and the Sea" is about the relationship between Santiago and the fish.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:17.220
It's kinda like "Moby Dick."  These sea movies, there's always about a guy and his fish fetish.

00:05:17.220 --> 00:05:19.560
"Making love to other gay fish!"

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:24.680
Anyway, if this was truly meant to be a parallel, there'd be more emphasis on the relationship with Manolin, his protégé.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:27.040
That would be the X23 stand-in, not the fish.

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:33.680
Plus-plus, why would a teaser for "Deadpool 2" be including a bunch of text about a movie we're about to see,

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:39.420
text that we won't be able to read in the theater so we have to watch the teaser again at home after we've already seen the movie?

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.300
Overall, it just wasn't the satisfying answer that I was looking for.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:48.500
So my initial theory, and where I started researching this whole thing was that maybe "The Old Man and the Sea" plot

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.720
is setting up themes that we're gonna see play out in "Deadpool 2,"

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:53.920
giving us a little preview of the plot, you know?

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.520
I mean, we know that Cable's gonna be in the movie, and he's kinda old and grouchy.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:02.060
So I searched through as many Deadpool comics as I could find, and none of his adventures with Cable

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.980
aligned with the key themes of "The Old Man and the Sea" either.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.740
And if we wanted to get REALLY literal with it, the only time he fights a big fish

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.780
is the villain Tiger Shark, but that's, um, that's just really stupid.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:15.080
So, what is it?  WHY IS IT THERE?

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:19.260
To give you a peek behind the scenes of the workings of this show, it's at this point when writing theories

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:25.880
that I start asking myself some really bizarre questions, and I start pursuing literally any interesting coincidence that I can find.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.680
Maybe this is a complete shot in the dark, but maybe out there somewhere

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:34.260
there's a random detail about Wade Wilson's life that he shares with Ernest Hemingway

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:36.300
and that'll lead to some other interesting stuff,

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:43.480
and oh boy!  I started researching down that path and was literally dumbfounded by just how deep those connections went.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:48.460
So even back then, hey, I decided to go through with an entire episode about him.  So here we are!

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:51.940
Maybe the merc with the mouth was really the merc with the pen--

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:56.060
or typewriter or whatever Hemingway wrote his garbage on.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:58.580
Again, not a big fan of Hemingway's writing.

00:06:58.580 --> 00:07:01.180
Submit it for the approval of the theorist society--

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:05.060
OH BOY!

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:09.960
Forget fighting Tiger Shark, I am full-on JUMPING the Tiger Shark for this one.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.980
So, to answer your first two questions, yes, I am completely serious,

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:15.280
and no, I have not recently consumed LSD.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.780
But I know some of you are sitting there at home with a couple of slam-dunk rebuttals right off the bat.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:25.340
What about the fact that we know exactly who Deadpool is, i.e. Wade Wilson?

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:28.880
Solid point, but dig a little deeper, and it starts to get complicated.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:34.860
One of Deadpool's antagonists, T-Ray, claims on many different occasions that he is the real Wade Wilson

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.640
and that Deadpool stole his identity.

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.140
Even when Deadpool is able to poke some holes in T-Ray's story,

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.280
he also admits that he can't quite be sure who he is

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.060
as evidenced by the fact that Deadpool's memories of his parents, i.e. that they both died while he was young,

00:07:48.060 --> 00:07:52.380
are actually quite different from what we see in Deadpool #34

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:57.180
when he actually burns his childhood home to the ground with his parents still inside.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:02.980
So let's just say that Deadpool may not be the most reliable of narrators of his own past.

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:09.040
In fact, the whole Deadpool/T-Ray identity mystery is actually a big thing that would make a great episode of COMIC THEORY!

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:10.820
Which, sadly isn't a thing that exists. . .

00:08:11.800 --> 00:08:12.300
yet!

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:17.060
And probably won't forever because comics and movies have so much crossover at this point (unless someone else does it),

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.440
and there's so much detail to it that it's not really worth going into here,

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:27.000
but, suffice to say, this unreliable memory coupled with the fact that Deadpool has died and been resurrected multiple times,

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.360
it gets really hard to say exactly who he is and where he came from.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.740
So now that that first rebuttal has one or two holes in it,

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:38.980
you might then contend to me that Ernest Hemingway is about as far away from the Marvel universe as you can get.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:42.880
Even if we don't know the true identity of who Deadpool really is,

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.580
we can't assume that this world contains real-life people like Ernest Hemingway in it.

00:08:47.580 --> 00:08:48.660
And you're totally right,

00:08:48.660 --> 00:08:54.560
except that we don't have to assume because Ernest Hemingway is a canon person in Marvel comics.

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:01.100
And not ONLY that, he's actually connected to Deadpool's dearest and bestest friend in the world,

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:05.240
they guy who got him his superpowers and the movie, Wolverine.

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:09.240
According to the comics, Hemingway and Wolvy hung out during the Spanish Civil War,

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.340
according to a three-issue arc from the 1980s.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.740
Does that necessarily prove that Hemingway IS Deadpool?

00:09:14.900 --> 00:09:16.100
No, of course not,

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:21.980
but does it prove that Hemingway is a character in the Marvel universe who could've plausibly turned into a superhero over time?

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.120
It absolutely opens the door to that sort of possibility.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:28.400
It's also worth comparing the personalities of the two.

00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:34.160
As I concluded in my previous theory, Deadpool enjoys controlling the narrative of his own comics, games, and movie,

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:39.400
soon to be movies, and tries to paint himself in as flattering and ---- kicking delight as possible.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:41.720
While Hemingway was mostly known for writing fiction,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:46.420
his autobiographical work shared the same desire for that self-promotion.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.860
One literary scholar wrote about Hemingway's memoir "A Moveable Feast" said that,

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:07.400
To put it differently, i.e. an academic not trying so hard to look smart,

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:12.040
Hemingway went out of his way to make himself look good and his enemies look bad.

00:10:12.040 --> 00:10:17.520
For example, in "A Moveable Feast," there's a passage in which the famous author F. Scott Fitzgerald

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:20.920
asked Hemingway regarding help with the size of his . . .

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:22.240
uh, how do I put it?

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:26.240
the size of his not-so-great-Gatsby, if you know what I'm sayin'

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:27.260
(distant voice: PENIS!)

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:33.400
an obvious knock to the masculinity of one of Hemingway's contemporaries and rivals (but also influences).

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:36.480
It's worth noting that Fitzgerald never disputed this passage,

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:40.620
but that's probably because he was already dead twenty-five years when it got published.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:43.180
In his fiction, Hemingway was famous for being concise,

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:48.220
but when it came to his own exploits, he was famously willing to embellish out the wazoo

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:50.360
in order to make himself seem superior to others.

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:54.920
From a stylistic standpoint, he and Deadpool, practically twinsies.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.360
And the similarities aren't just isolated to the personalities of the two,

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:00.520
they also extend to the people around them.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:03.920
For instance, one of Deadpool's greatest allies is Cable,

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.220
a cybernetic future mutant he's fought alongside and has even merged his DNA with.

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.320
So they're practically genetically-linked family.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:15.780
One of Cable's plots involves building an island in the Pacific Ocean out of an old ship

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:19.340
on which he hoped to create a Utopian society called Providence.

00:11:19.340 --> 00:11:24.920
Of course, Providence is eventually destroyed by evil aliens, because ain't that always the way with Utopian societies?

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:29.740
But in a truly bizarre twist of fate, Ernest Hemingway's brother--

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.760
his brother, so again a partner-in-life who's connected through DNA--

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.580
did almost the exact same thing!

00:11:36.660 --> 00:11:41.540
Leicester Hemingway decided to create his own island nation in the 1960s,

00:11:41.540 --> 00:11:46.560
which he did by stringing together large rafts and weighing them down with an engine block

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:48.820
only a few miles off the coast of Jamaica.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:54.700
He actually did this, and although this might sound like the plan of someone whose wardrobe consists mostly of aluminum foil,

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:57.440
it was generally an organized operation.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:03.420
The island, which he called New Atlantis, had a flag, it issued postage, it even wrote a constitution

00:12:03.420 --> 00:12:08.640
before it was destroyed by storms just a few years, YEARS, after its creation.

00:12:08.640 --> 00:12:13.460
Granted, this isn't direct proof that Deadpool are connected just because they share a guy

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:18.880
who they're also genetically linked related to in some capacity who decided to do this random island experiment,

00:12:18.880 --> 00:12:22.300
But what are the freaking odds of that one?

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:24.720
How many comic book arcs are there of man-made islands? *ding!*

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.940
How many instances of man-made islands are there in real life? *ding!*

00:12:27.940 --> 00:12:32.720
And how many of those two instances are crossing over in each other's movie trailers? *ding!*

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:38.660
But the comparisons between Deadpool and Hemingway start to get REALLY interesting when you start to look at their biographical similarities.

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:40.800
For one, they were both skilled military types.

00:12:40.800 --> 00:12:43.960
Deadpool was originally a member of the U.S. Army's Special Forces,

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:49.720
but as someone unwilling to follow orders and protocol, he found much more success in his para-military mercinary work

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:50.960
for which we all know and love him.

00:12:50.960 --> 00:12:55.740
Hemingway also wanted to be part of the military, attempting to enlist to fight in World War I,

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:58.080
but they turned him away because of his poor eyesight.

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:03.140
He still had a desire for combat, though, so Hemingway ended up as an ambulance driver in World War I.

00:13:03.140 --> 00:13:06.780
And in the early stages of World War II, when Hemingway was living in Cuba,

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:12.840
he convinced the Cuban government to let him outfit his fishing boat with machine guns and hand grenades

00:13:12.840 --> 00:13:15.580
so that he could attack any German U-boats that he spotted.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:19.160
Fishing boat!  Bad ---, this guy was crazy!

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:23.060
We also know that both Deadpool and Hemingway are dangerous in hand-to-hand combat.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:28.020
In Cable and Deadpool #36, Deadpool manages to defeat another mercenary named Taskmaster

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:30.680
mano-a-mano with his hands chained together.

00:13:30.680 --> 00:13:33.560
Similarly, Hemingway was notorious as a brawler.

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:38.280
Heavyweight boxing champ of the world in the 1920s Jack Dempsey was once quoted as saying,

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:48.260
"I had this sense that Hemingway, who really thought he could box, would come out of the corner like a mad man."

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:52.200
So Dempsey's full comments make it clear that he didn't think Hemingway would've won necessarily,

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:58.820
but if you're an amateur boxer, and the heavyweight champion of the world doesn't want to fight you because he thinks you're crazy,

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.960
well, you must be pretty ---- crazy.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:06.500
The details here are obviously different, but the personality and abilities share a lot of similarities.

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:12.660
We've got two people who crave combat but prefer to do it under their own terms and aren't afraid to mix it up

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.360
in a CRAZY, off-the-wall style if the need arises.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:19.440
But I'm sure a lot of you probably still aren't satisfied, and I don't blame you.

00:14:19.440 --> 00:14:23.700
Contrary to what many of you commenters think, I actually do have some level of self-awareness,

00:14:23.700 --> 00:14:28.080
and I tend to know when I made a good theory verses a bad one, or, actually probably more accurately,

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.700
one that I'm convinced of verses one that I'm like, erm, there's still a lot of holes in this thing.

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:38.120
Surprise, surprise! Just because I do an episode on something doesn't necessarily mean I believe it myself.

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:44.120
I just try to make as strong a case as possible, and if I think I've a decent job of it, yeah, it becomes a full-length episode.

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:47.420
And sure, Hemingway and Deadpool have some similar personality traits,

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:51.900
some similarities in temperament and physical prowess, were canonically present in the comics,

00:14:51.900 --> 00:14:57.060
and yeah, even share some crazy similarities in the actions of the people who are genetically linked to them.

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:00.120
But there is still a pretty big problem here.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:05.140
All of this is still coincidental.  The details just don't lock perfectly together.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:08.060
You know, they're similar, but they're not exact.

00:15:08.280 --> 00:15:14.260
